# evidence-for: US-005, FR-012
# evidence-timeout: 120
#
# D-107. GI-002 has start.md and resume.md run install-commit-guard.sh at the
# top of every run against every target repo, so whatever this installer does
# to a repository it does before any casting begins.
#
# It did this. `cp` and `chmod` FOLLOW a symlink at the destination: they act
# on the file the link points AT and leave the link itself standing. The layout
# under test is the ordinary way a project version-controls its hooks
# (husky/lefthook-style): the hook body is a tracked file in the work tree and
# .git/hooks/pre-commit is a relative symlink into it. Against that layout
# every branch of the installer's `case "$ACTION"` block rewrote a TRACKED
# file, left the hook unreplaced, and reported success.
#
# The PRE-FIX block is that, measured. The four rows have to be read together:
# the tracked file is rewritten, the target repo's working tree is dirty, the
# hook git will actually run is STILL the original symlink -- and the installer
# printed "Commit guard active" over all of it. The "hook is the shipped guard,
# executable: yes" row is not a contradiction, it is the mechanism. `cmp` and
# `-x` resolve through the same link, so the installer's own post-install
# verification confirmed the clobber instead of catching it. A dirty tracked
# file nobody wrote on purpose is then exactly what the next teammate's
# pathspec commit can capture.
#
# The FIXED block installs by writing a temp file inside the hooks directory
# and renaming it into place. rename(2) replaces the LINK, never what it points
# at, so the tracked file keeps its bytes and the working tree stays clean.
# And because a symlink is now foreign by construction -- this installer only
# ever copies, so it cannot have produced a link, whatever bytes sit at the far
# end -- the operator is told what was replaced, what it resolved to, and how
# to put it back. The backup is taken with `cp -P` so it preserves the LINK:
# a dereferenced backup would restore a copy of the script into a place where
# the project deliberately keeps a pointer, and the old "Restore with" command
# wrote through the link a second time, restoring the wrong file entirely.
#
# The dangling-link rows close the hole that made `-e` the wrong test. `-e`
# follows the link, so a pre-commit pointing at a path that does not exist read
# as "nothing is here" and took the plain install branch -- where `cp` refuses
# to write through a dangling symlink on both GNU and BSD, so `set -e` aborted
# on a bare `cp:` error naming no cause, in violation of this script's own exit
# contract, leaving the repo unguarded. `-L` is true for live and dangling
# links alike, and is now tested first.
#
# The last block re-drives the plain-file paths TEMPER verified as not-broken
# (a foreign hook backed up and loudly replaced, a second run idempotent),
# because the fix routes every write through one helper and those paths go
# through it too.
#
# Both installers run from the same fixture builder against freshly initialised
# repositories, so the installer is the only variable between the two blocks.
# The pre-fix one is read out of git at a pinned SHA rather than reconstructed
# by hand. Nothing volatile is printed -- no temp path, no backup timestamp,
# no duration -- so there is nothing to declare.

